  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1019 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nOver the weekend, a quick pattern transition will take place, with\nthe 5h and 8h ridges shift east of the region. This will allow a\nshortwave to enter the Ozarks and Mid South on Saturday into Sunday.\nThe greatest UVVs will be on Sunday for the local area with our\ngreatest shower chances. Only a slight chance of a thunderstorm\nexists at this point with limited instability. QPF of 0.4-1.3 inches\nwith the highest amounts west of I-65 looks OK for now. With the\nsurface/wedging remaining across the Lee of the Appalachians into the\nsoutheast U.S., a cool and fairly stable lower atmosphere is\nexpected. Chances wane late Sunday, with low chances of light rain \nor perhaps some drizzle look favorable given shallow isentropic \nascent over the cooler airmass that arrives. By Monday into Tuesday,\nminor upper ridging takes places and shifts into the Southeast U.S.\nahead of a strong deepening upper trough in the Plains states.\nSoutherly flow will increase Monday into Tuesday. A line of showers\nand thunderstorms will take shape in the lower MS Valley Tuesday \nin advance of the trough and cold front. But the deepening and \nslow nature of the upper low may delay the arrival of widespread \nprecipitation until Wednesday.",
